1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,440
One possibility, of course, is that they're doing it

2
00:00:04,440 --> 00:00:06,240
to impress their friends, right?

3
00:00:06,240 --> 00:00:10,080
This is the Robin Hanson theory about almost all that's going

4
00:00:10,080 --> 00:00:13,260
on is, let me just do it so people don't hassle you.

5
00:00:13,660 --> 00:00:15,440
That does sort of just push it back.

6
00:00:15,440 --> 00:00:18,440
Well, why are people out on you for failing to do something

7
00:00:18,440 --> 00:00:21,600
that where there's no actual benefit to you or them, in fact?

8
00:00:22,160 --> 00:00:25,600
The other main theory is the civic duty theory

9
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:27,320
that many people feel like some kind

10
00:00:27,320 --> 00:00:30,440
of content idea I should do it regardless of the consequences.

11
00:00:30,440 --> 00:00:32,800
Although this combines with a weird agnosticism

12
00:00:32,800 --> 00:00:33,940
about who you should vote for.

13
00:00:33,940 --> 00:00:35,940
So, you know, the most common, so there's a book

14
00:00:35,940 --> 00:00:38,200
by Jason Foss where Jason Bender called The Ethics of Voting

15
00:00:38,620 --> 00:00:41,160
where he talks about what he calls the common sense theory

16
00:00:41,160 --> 00:00:43,640
of voting which says it's incredibly morally important

17
00:00:43,640 --> 00:00:44,940
that you vote for somebody or other,

18
00:00:45,320 --> 00:00:47,400
but it makes almost no moral difference at all

19
00:00:47,400 --> 00:00:48,100
who you vote for.

20
00:00:49,180 --> 00:00:50,700
And that, he points out in the book,

21
00:00:50,700 --> 00:00:52,940
is a very strange theory, right?

22
00:00:52,940 --> 00:00:56,900
Akin to a bunch of people being in a surgical room

23
00:00:56,900 --> 00:00:59,740
and saying, look, it doesn't really matter where you chop

24
00:00:59,740 --> 00:01:01,860
into this person, but it's very important

25
00:01:01,860 --> 00:01:03,080
that you participate in the surgery.

26
00:01:04,240 --> 00:01:05,980
Just pick up a scalpel and do something.

27
00:01:05,980 --> 00:01:08,060
That's what's important.

28
00:01:08,060 --> 00:01:10,660
Isn't that completely reversed?

29
00:01:10,960 --> 00:01:13,040
Isn't the only important thing that you do it right?

30
00:01:13,500 --> 00:01:15,200
And if you don't know what you're doing,

31
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:17,740
the important thing that you not participate and get

32
00:01:17,740 --> 00:01:21,040
out of the way of people who can actually save lives?

33
00:01:21,660 --> 00:01:23,780
But, and he runs with this in the book,

34
00:01:23,780 --> 00:01:26,260
but Jason Bender's view remains a minority position,

35
00:01:26,260 --> 00:01:28,180
strangely, despite overpowering logic.

36
00:01:29,180 --> 00:01:29,440
All right.

37
00:01:30,380 --> 00:01:32,540
And he's doing well in Germany, so somehow.

38
00:01:32,540 --> 00:01:36,460
He's going to be the mayor of Potsdam,

39
00:01:36,580 --> 00:01:37,580
invited him to give a talk.

40
00:01:38,080 --> 00:01:40,220
A keynote address, in fact.

41
00:01:40,360 --> 00:01:42,400
So, it's a weird world.

42
00:01:42,400 --> 00:01:46,440
It just keeps getting stranger.

43
00:01:46,980 --> 00:01:47,540
All right.

44
00:01:47,880 --> 00:01:50,120
Anyway, why do they call it the miracle?

45
00:01:50,380 --> 00:01:52,120
Well, if you've ever heard of medieval alchemy

46
00:01:52,120 --> 00:01:54,860
where you say, okay, we take 90% lead, 10% gold,

47
00:01:54,860 --> 00:01:58,360
hocus pocus, and then boom, pure gold.

48
00:01:58,820 --> 00:01:59,980
That would be a miracle, right?

49
00:01:59,980 --> 00:02:01,020
And it also doesn't work.

50
00:02:01,740 --> 00:02:05,600
But this is something that if the assumptions are true,

51
00:02:05,600 --> 00:02:06,300
it does work.

52
00:02:06,700 --> 00:02:10,180
You really could, in theory, actually get a democracy

53
00:02:10,180 --> 00:02:12,040
where almost no one is informed,

54
00:02:12,360 --> 00:02:15,100
and yet it does exactly what a perfectly informed electorate

55
00:02:15,100 --> 00:02:15,300
would do.

56
00:02:15,300 --> 00:02:18,040
Okay. All right.

57
00:02:18,300 --> 00:02:19,020
So, I guess.

58
00:02:19,020 --> 00:02:26,780
Yes. There do have to be some informed people.

59
00:02:26,780 --> 00:02:27,300
That is correct.

60
00:02:27,300 --> 00:02:34,460
So, well, so it's not consistent

61
00:02:34,460 --> 00:02:37,420
with 100% rational ignorance, but it is consistent

62
00:02:37,420 --> 00:02:39,600
with, say, 90% rational ignorance.

63
00:02:40,200 --> 00:02:42,080
Right? And the real, you know, if we're being empirical,

64
00:02:42,560 --> 00:02:44,980
you should never expect things to be 100% perfect.

65
00:02:45,680 --> 00:02:48,160
Right? Actually, we should move on a bit because we have

66
00:02:48,160 --> 00:02:49,360
so much to get through.

67
00:02:49,360 --> 00:02:51,160
But if you're really fascinated, of course,

68
00:02:51,160 --> 00:02:52,420
there's always taking the full class.

69
00:02:52,560 --> 00:02:54,200
There's 14 weeks for questions.

70
00:02:54,400 --> 00:02:55,420
All right.

71
00:02:55,800 --> 00:02:59,140
Now, you can analyze this politician-voter relationship

72
00:02:59,140 --> 00:03:00,500
just like a principal agent problem.

73
00:03:00,860 --> 00:03:02,480
Remember, we talked about principal agent problems.

74
00:03:03,140 --> 00:03:06,720
And I think we even talked about the probability of punishment

75
00:03:06,720 --> 00:03:08,560
and the severity of punishment at some point.

76
00:03:09,180 --> 00:03:11,900
So, simple model just says the politicians will do what voters

77
00:03:11,900 --> 00:03:15,240
want, and the benefit of doing what voters want is greater

78
00:03:15,240 --> 00:03:16,580
than the benefit of shirking.

79
00:03:16,580 --> 00:03:19,060
Remember, shirking is just the politician doing whatever he

80
00:03:19,060 --> 00:03:20,700
wants for whatever reason he wants.

81
00:03:21,100 --> 00:03:22,200
It could be they're lazy.

82
00:03:22,440 --> 00:03:23,400
Could be they're corrupt.

83
00:03:23,740 --> 00:03:26,420
Could be that they just disagree with their supporters.

84
00:03:26,900 --> 00:03:27,820
All possibilities.

85
00:03:27,860 --> 00:03:29,800
But anyway, whatever that is, we call shirking,

86
00:03:30,200 --> 00:03:32,900
minus the probability of punishment times the severity

87
00:03:32,900 --> 00:03:34,080
of punishment if you do something bad.

88
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:39,760
Now, if you go and put in a really low probability,

89
00:03:40,540 --> 00:03:43,300
then it pretty much looks like politicians, like that rounds

90
00:03:43,300 --> 00:03:46,200
down to zero, and then politicians always do whatever

91
00:03:46,200 --> 00:03:47,760
they feel like and ignore voters.

92
00:03:48,360 --> 00:03:48,560
All right?

93
00:03:49,180 --> 00:03:52,360
So, it seems like voter rational ignorance implies

94
00:03:52,800 --> 00:03:55,340
that so-called democratically elected politicians can do

95
00:03:55,340 --> 00:03:56,320
almost anything they want.

96
00:03:57,600 --> 00:04:00,200
But Gary Becker observed that there's an algebra

97
00:04:00,200 --> 00:04:00,980
that can save the world.

98
00:04:01,500 --> 00:04:01,980
All right?

99
00:04:01,980 --> 00:04:05,740
And the algebra that can save the world is to go and put D

100
00:04:05,740 --> 00:04:09,340
over on a resistance to solve for D and get the result

101
00:04:09,340 --> 00:04:12,220
that we just need to make D, which is the damages

102
00:04:12,220 --> 00:04:15,380
or the punishment that we inflict upon corrupt politicians

103
00:04:15,380 --> 00:04:17,020
or shirking politicians when we catch them.

104
00:04:17,540 --> 00:04:20,980
And that just needs to be equal to benefit of shirking minus

105
00:04:21,400 --> 00:04:22,700
benefit of doing what voters want.

106
00:04:23,100 --> 00:04:26,660
Divide all that by P, and we then impose that.

107
00:04:27,480 --> 00:04:30,540
Right? So, if you were caught on camera taking a $1 bribe,

108
00:04:31,280 --> 00:04:33,480
voters could either say, well, what are we going to do?

109
00:04:33,800 --> 00:04:36,700
Or, because now, if we catch them, when we,

110
00:04:36,700 --> 00:04:39,380
the few times we catch them, we need to rain hellfire

111
00:04:39,420 --> 00:04:41,580
down upon them, and then they would then,

112
00:04:41,820 --> 00:04:43,360
this will keep a lot of them honest.

113
00:04:43,360 --> 00:04:46,040
It's like any time your parents ever caught you doing something

114
00:04:46,040 --> 00:04:47,640
bad, and then they harshly punish you,

115
00:04:47,640 --> 00:04:50,060
anyone ever been harshly punished for a small offense

116
00:04:50,060 --> 00:04:53,340
by their parents, and then you whine and say, it's not fair.

117
00:04:53,800 --> 00:04:56,400
And then, if your parents are Beckerian,

118
00:04:56,680 --> 00:04:58,980
they will say, I'm also punishing you

119
00:04:58,980 --> 00:05:00,100
for all the time that I didn't catch you.

120
00:05:01,280 --> 00:05:03,240
Right? So, I need to give a really harsh punishment

121
00:05:03,240 --> 00:05:05,000
when I do catch you, because it's so hard to catch you.

122
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:05,660
You're so sneaky.

123
00:05:06,400 --> 00:05:09,140
And, therefore, you will be afraid to go

124
00:05:09,140 --> 00:05:12,420
and break the rules, because a small chance

125
00:05:12,420 --> 00:05:14,880
of getting caught with a horrible punishment,

126
00:05:14,880 --> 00:05:17,260
if you lose your car for a year, won't be worth it.

127
00:05:18,320 --> 00:05:21,080
Okay. Now, what's interesting about this is it does seem

128
00:05:21,080 --> 00:05:24,040
to me that, theoretically, voters do have a way

129
00:05:24,520 --> 00:05:25,540
of controlling politicians,

130
00:05:25,540 --> 00:05:26,920
even when they are highly ignorant,

131
00:05:27,340 --> 00:05:31,380
which is just to very harshly punish whatever bad information

132
00:05:31,980 --> 00:05:32,980
is revealed.

133
00:05:33,760 --> 00:05:35,940
Right? Now, again, of course, if P is literally zero,

134
00:05:35,940 --> 00:05:36,900
then this is not a solution.

135
00:05:37,520 --> 00:05:38,700
Then you need an infinite punishment.

136
00:05:38,700 --> 00:05:40,140
You can't inflict an infinite punishment.

137
00:05:40,140 --> 00:05:42,740
But it means that as long as there is some chance

138
00:05:43,140 --> 00:05:45,400
of accidental discovery,

139
00:05:45,940 --> 00:05:48,900
and in the real world there's always a chance to get caught.

140
00:05:49,500 --> 00:05:50,000
Right? Yeah.

141
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:55,080
So, any Wikipedia article you read about a smart criminal,

142
00:05:55,480 --> 00:05:56,740
well, why are they in Wikipedia?

143
00:05:56,740 --> 00:05:59,220
Because they got caught, and they messed something up.

144
00:05:59,880 --> 00:06:02,140
Right? So, and similarly with the politicians.

145
00:06:02,140 --> 00:06:04,540
Like, no one will ever discover that I'm cheating

146
00:06:04,540 --> 00:06:05,180
with an intern.

147
00:06:05,420 --> 00:06:06,020
How could they?

148
00:06:06,020 --> 00:06:08,620
Right? Or, of course, there's the Richard Dixon.

149
00:06:08,620 --> 00:06:12,900
How would anyone ever find out that I'm covering up Watergate?

150
00:06:13,400 --> 00:06:13,820
Oh, right.

151
00:06:13,820 --> 00:06:15,820
I forgot that I tape record all my conversations.

152
00:06:15,820 --> 00:06:16,560
No. Hmm.

153
00:06:16,560 --> 00:06:20,320
Wasn't someone turning off the tapes?

154
00:06:20,720 --> 00:06:21,900
No. Right?

155
00:06:21,900 --> 00:06:25,220
And then, you know, so that's the actual story.

156
00:06:25,720 --> 00:06:27,880
Then you try releasing it heavily redacted,

157
00:06:27,880 --> 00:06:32,500
but then you can't just black out the entire transcript

158
00:06:32,780 --> 00:06:33,620
and say I complied.

159
00:06:34,680 --> 00:06:35,180
All right.

160
00:06:35,180 --> 00:06:40,760
Now, let us then move on to my great nemesis.

161
00:06:40,760 --> 00:06:43,260
I have several nemeses, but Donald Whitman is my first one,

162
00:06:43,260 --> 00:06:43,860
my first love.

163
00:06:44,360 --> 00:06:45,360
All right.

164
00:06:45,820 --> 00:06:46,320
All right.

165
00:06:46,540 --> 00:06:48,920
Just as a matter of strategy, it's always good

166
00:06:48,920 --> 00:06:50,420
to have an intellectual nemesis.

167
00:06:50,800 --> 00:06:52,380
And the same reason that you don't want

168
00:06:52,380 --> 00:06:53,920
to be the only lawyer in a town.

169
00:06:54,420 --> 00:06:56,140
There should be at least two lawyers in every town.

170
00:06:56,660 --> 00:07:00,920
That way you can have clients and take cases

171
00:07:00,920 --> 00:07:02,180
against each other and always eat

172
00:07:02,180 --> 00:07:03,360
in the best restaurant every day.

173
00:07:03,360 --> 00:07:05,120
Right? In your small town.

174
00:07:05,420 --> 00:07:06,640
So you want there to be a conflict

175
00:07:06,640 --> 00:07:08,540
because conflict is entertaining for outsiders.

176
00:07:09,100 --> 00:07:10,660
So you want to find, you know, but of course you want

177
00:07:10,660 --> 00:07:11,620
to pick your nemesis well.

178
00:07:11,620 --> 00:07:13,040
You don't want to get a jerk.

179
00:07:13,460 --> 00:07:16,680
You want to get an entertaining and brilliant adversary

180
00:07:16,680 --> 00:07:18,080
so that you both make each other look good.

181
00:07:18,860 --> 00:07:19,360
All right.

182
00:07:19,360 --> 00:07:21,120
And make it seem like you're both more important

183
00:07:21,120 --> 00:07:22,760
than you would appear to be singly.

184
00:07:23,240 --> 00:07:24,220
Right? Just talking to yourself.

185
00:07:24,220 --> 00:07:25,300
You're just a person talking to yourself.

186
00:07:25,300 --> 00:07:26,240
But a debate.

187
00:07:26,580 --> 00:07:29,480
My God, there's two smart people who disagree about something

188
00:07:29,480 --> 00:07:31,220
and they both have arguments.

189
00:07:31,540 --> 00:07:33,280
And now I've got to participate in this.

190
00:07:33,280 --> 00:07:34,280
And hear what's going on.

191
00:07:34,280 --> 00:07:35,420
Whereas otherwise it's like, well,

192
00:07:35,420 --> 00:07:36,480
I was the person proving things.

193
00:07:36,480 --> 00:07:37,780
That's much more boring.

194
00:07:38,780 --> 00:07:40,280
So anyway.

195
00:07:40,280 --> 00:07:43,360
So Donald Whitman, a professor at UC Santa Cruz.

196
00:07:43,880 --> 00:07:47,560
Most people who don't like public choice

197
00:07:47,560 --> 00:07:51,240
and the economic approach to politics in general dislike,

198
00:07:51,240 --> 00:07:53,140
first of all, the economistic assumptions,

199
00:07:53,140 --> 00:07:55,080
thinking about politics in an economistic way.

200
00:07:55,080 --> 00:07:58,120
And they also just don't like the anti-democratic conclusions.

201
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:01,780
You might just say like the not so positive

202
00:08:01,780 --> 00:08:03,040
about democracy conclusions.

203
00:08:03,040 --> 00:08:04,840
It's not me, is it?

204
00:08:04,840 --> 00:08:06,580
It's not the buzzing?

205
00:08:06,580 --> 00:08:07,840
Can someone stop the buzzing?

206
00:08:07,840 --> 00:08:08,840
All right.

207
00:08:08,840 --> 00:08:10,940
Okay. So anyway.

208
00:08:10,940 --> 00:08:15,360
This is a broad and fertile tradition of just complaining

209
00:08:15,360 --> 00:08:18,360
about economists shouldn't be mucking up other fields

210
00:08:18,360 --> 00:08:20,560
with their already dubious approach.

211
00:08:20,560 --> 00:08:26,800
In the wall?

212
00:08:26,800 --> 00:08:30,100
All right.

213
00:08:30,100 --> 00:08:33,160
Okay. All right.

214
00:08:33,320 --> 00:08:34,620
So anyway.

215
00:08:34,620 --> 00:08:36,240
So that is a rich tradition.

216
00:08:36,240 --> 00:08:40,580
And though I may not seem to take it seriously,

217
00:08:40,580 --> 00:08:41,920
I've learned plenty of things

218
00:08:41,920 --> 00:08:43,800
from the anti-economistic tradition.

219
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:45,920
But that's not what Donald Whitman is.

220
00:08:45,980 --> 00:08:48,980
Donald Whitman is an economist economist

221
00:08:49,340 --> 00:08:51,620
who says something much more hurtful to economists

222
00:08:51,620 --> 00:08:53,320
than you, you're being a bunch of economists.

223
00:08:53,420 --> 00:08:54,320
Being economist-y.

224
00:08:54,860 --> 00:08:57,760
Instead, he says the ultimate insult to economists.

225
00:08:57,760 --> 00:09:00,320
You are bad economists.

226
00:09:00,620 --> 00:09:03,360
You guys are giving economics a bad name

227
00:09:03,860 --> 00:09:06,720
with your illogical application of our principles.

228
00:09:06,720 --> 00:09:09,260
And that's very hurtful.

229
00:09:10,220 --> 00:09:10,400
All right.

230
00:09:10,400 --> 00:09:12,420
So he doesn't complain about the economist approach.

231
00:09:12,860 --> 00:09:14,380
Says that there's a lot of public choices,

232
00:09:14,380 --> 00:09:15,460
very bad economics.

233
00:09:16,180 --> 00:09:17,840
And in particular, he says

234
00:09:17,900 --> 00:09:20,500
that the standard public choice arguments,

235
00:09:20,500 --> 00:09:24,140
many of which you probably heard said probably kind

236
00:09:24,140 --> 00:09:24,740
of informally.

237
00:09:24,740 --> 00:09:54,620
So what Whitman says is that if you look

238
00:09:54,620 --> 00:09:56,600
closely, if you look closely,

239
00:09:57,820 --> 00:10:00,340
what public choice economists are usually doing is not

240
00:10:00,340 --> 00:10:01,860
actually normal economics.

241
00:10:02,320 --> 00:10:04,600
Instead, they're doing much normal stuff.

242
00:10:04,600 --> 00:10:07,540
And then they sneak in an assumption

243
00:10:07,540 --> 00:10:09,180
that most economists would not like

244
00:10:09,180 --> 00:10:10,880
and that the public choice economists themselves would

245
00:10:10,880 --> 00:10:11,500
object to.

246
00:10:11,820 --> 00:10:12,600
You see it explicitly.

247
00:10:12,600 --> 00:10:15,920
They smuggle in a dubious assumption.

248
00:10:16,300 --> 00:10:18,500
And that's how they actually reach the interesting

249
00:10:18,500 --> 00:10:18,920
conclusions.

250
00:10:20,100 --> 00:10:23,800
Right. And if they didn't have these dubious assumptions,

251
00:10:23,800 --> 00:10:26,600
then they would be stuck just saying the world works

252
00:10:26,600 --> 00:10:27,020
and it's fine.

253
00:10:28,540 --> 00:10:30,040
So what are the assumptions

254
00:10:30,040 --> 00:10:32,040
that Whitman accuses public choice economists

255
00:10:32,040 --> 00:10:33,500
of smuggling in?

256
00:10:34,080 --> 00:10:36,560
So the first one he just calls extreme voter stupidity.

257
00:10:40,880 --> 00:10:43,620
Right. And he uses that word stupidity very deliberately,

258
00:10:44,040 --> 00:10:45,440
saying no, no, not ignorance.

259
00:10:45,560 --> 00:10:48,440
Ignorance isn't enough to get you where you're going.

260
00:10:49,040 --> 00:10:52,320
You actually need to think of voters as dummies, as morons.

261
00:10:52,320 --> 00:10:54,680
Right. People who just aren't applying common sense.

262
00:10:55,480 --> 00:10:56,700
And then you can get results.

263
00:10:57,280 --> 00:10:59,080
But of course, normally an economist doesn't like

264
00:10:59,080 --> 00:11:01,420
to begin a model with, all right, here's my first assumption.

265
00:11:02,040 --> 00:11:03,080
People don't have common sense.

266
00:11:03,080 --> 00:11:05,720
Right. People are utterly lacking common sense.

267
00:11:05,720 --> 00:11:07,040
That's where I begin.

268
00:11:07,600 --> 00:11:09,300
Right. Normally economists don't like that assumption.

269
00:11:09,300 --> 00:11:11,100
And Whitman says that's what you're really doing,

270
00:11:11,540 --> 00:11:13,140
assuming you lack the common sense among voters.

271
00:11:13,800 --> 00:11:15,420
Right. So that's one assumption.

272
00:11:15,720 --> 00:11:17,500
Second one he calls serious lack of competition.

273
00:11:17,500 --> 00:11:19,100
Normally you can just put that in as monopoly.

274
00:11:19,100 --> 00:11:21,760
It's the idea that while politics on the surface looks

275
00:11:21,760 --> 00:11:25,840
like a battle royale between a whole bunch

276
00:11:25,840 --> 00:11:29,340
of people struggling for power, actually it's just a big,

277
00:11:29,340 --> 00:11:30,640
collusive organization.

278
00:11:30,640 --> 00:11:33,640
So if you ever heard of the Demo-Republican Party,

279
00:11:33,640 --> 00:11:36,440
it's like a one-party state where they all work hand

280
00:11:36,440 --> 00:11:38,620
and glove in order to deny the people what they want.

281
00:11:39,240 --> 00:11:41,920
So that's another story Whitman says is getting smuggled in,

282
00:11:42,420 --> 00:11:44,920
is somehow competition has been suppressed

283
00:11:44,920 --> 00:11:47,760
and things are just rammed down the voter's brain.

284
00:11:47,760 --> 00:11:49,720
Things are just rammed down the voter's throat whether

285
00:11:49,720 --> 00:11:50,320
like it or not.

286
00:11:50,320 --> 00:11:52,520
And nobody is around to say, hey,

287
00:11:52,520 --> 00:11:53,520
I've never treated you that way.

288
00:11:54,820 --> 00:11:56,260
And then the last assumption

289
00:11:56,260 --> 00:11:58,900
that he accuses public choice economists of smuggling in,

290
00:11:58,900 --> 00:12:01,440
it's what he calls excessively high negotiations

291
00:12:01,440 --> 00:12:02,300
as transfer costs.

292
00:12:02,880 --> 00:12:04,460
It's called high transactions costs.

293
00:12:04,820 --> 00:12:05,200
It's easy.

294
00:12:05,960 --> 00:12:06,180
All right.

295
00:12:06,180 --> 00:12:09,640
And Whitman says as long as we avoid all these,

296
00:12:09,640 --> 00:12:13,580
as long as we wipe out any hint of these assumptions

297
00:12:13,580 --> 00:12:16,380
from public choice, we're not going to get any

298
00:12:16,380 --> 00:12:18,640
of the results about how democracy is efficient

299
00:12:18,640 --> 00:12:19,260
or anything else.

300
00:12:19,680 --> 00:12:21,520
We're going to get a very functionalist account

301
00:12:21,880 --> 00:12:23,120
of the world is fine.

302
00:12:23,920 --> 00:12:25,860
The world is counter-exfficient.

303
00:12:26,700 --> 00:12:30,120
And it's a very strong kind of efficiency where

304
00:12:30,620 --> 00:12:33,440
in Whitman's view we leave to democracy the things

305
00:12:33,440 --> 00:12:35,540
that are best left to democracy and we leave to the markets the

306
00:12:35,540 --> 00:12:36,720
things that are best left to market

307
00:12:36,720 --> 00:12:39,900
so we have an optimal allocation of all tasks in society.

308
00:12:41,540 --> 00:12:45,800
Of course, I think Whitman would still be happy to say, look,

309
00:12:45,800 --> 00:12:48,040
it's perfectly fine for there

310
00:12:48,040 --> 00:12:51,960
to be an occasional temporary brief disequilibrium,

311
00:12:51,960 --> 00:12:53,220
but then the system will catch up.

312
00:12:53,920 --> 00:12:54,800
So don't worry about it.

313
00:12:54,800 --> 00:12:55,560
Everything's going to be fine.

314
00:12:56,700 --> 00:12:56,900
All right.

315
00:12:57,400 --> 00:12:58,920
So and then the implication

316
00:12:58,920 --> 00:13:02,000
of this is all the public choice complaining you've heard

317
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:03,480
about democracy at George Mason,

318
00:13:03,480 --> 00:13:05,640
or probably before you came here given that you're here,

319
00:13:05,960 --> 00:13:09,860
all that complaining is just a bunch of losers whining

320
00:13:09,920 --> 00:13:13,420
that their preferences are actually very unpopular.

321
00:13:13,420 --> 00:13:17,960
So it's akin to someone saying, you know,

322
00:13:17,960 --> 00:13:20,760
there's a giant market failure in music that nobody,

323
00:13:20,760 --> 00:13:22,600
that I'm not Britney Spears.

324
00:13:23,720 --> 00:13:25,960
It's like, yeah, that's the market failure

325
00:13:25,960 --> 00:13:27,080
that a person who can't sing

326
00:13:27,080 --> 00:13:29,600
and can't dance is not popular.

327
00:13:30,280 --> 00:13:31,940
I mean, what do you know?

328
00:13:32,340 --> 00:13:36,360
Right? How about another theory that you're not any good

329
00:13:36,360 --> 00:13:38,100
and you're losing for that reason

330
00:13:38,100 --> 00:13:40,320
and then you're concocting an elaborate conspiracy theory

331
00:13:40,320 --> 00:13:41,780
to explain your failure?

332
00:13:41,780 --> 00:13:45,460
That is essentially what Whitman is pointing

333
00:13:45,460 --> 00:13:47,560
at people in public choice and saying, well, look,

334
00:13:47,560 --> 00:13:48,380
you guys are weird.

335
00:13:48,380 --> 00:13:51,740
You're generally libertarians, free market type people.

336
00:13:51,740 --> 00:13:53,380
You're mad that you're losers.

337
00:13:53,880 --> 00:13:56,620
And I'd be mad too if I were one of you.

338
00:13:56,620 --> 00:13:58,520
Yes, it's not fun to lose all the time

339
00:13:58,520 --> 00:14:00,260
when your views are hopelessly outnumbered.

340
00:14:00,720 --> 00:14:02,920
But there's nothing inefficient about it.

341
00:14:02,920 --> 00:14:03,760
It's just that you're weird.

342
00:14:04,100 --> 00:14:05,140
And weird people don't get their way.

343
00:14:05,400 --> 00:14:05,800
Watch your thing.

344
00:14:06,900 --> 00:14:07,080
All right.

345
00:14:07,080 --> 00:14:07,960
So that's the heart of it.

346
00:14:08,760 --> 00:14:08,940
All right.

347
00:14:08,940 --> 00:14:15,300
Now let's just quickly go through Whitman's arguments

348
00:14:15,300 --> 00:14:16,720
for each of these or where he's coming from.

349
00:14:17,260 --> 00:14:17,440
All right.

350
00:14:17,440 --> 00:14:18,520
Now just very quick.

351
00:14:18,520 --> 00:14:21,400
You may doubt whether Whitman's nemesis can actually fairly

352
00:14:22,060 --> 00:14:25,680
present his views, but I am certified by Whitman himself.

353
00:14:26,440 --> 00:14:30,160
So in an exchange with him, he began by saying, usually

354
00:14:30,160 --> 00:14:31,260
when I argue with my opponents,

355
00:14:31,260 --> 00:14:32,740
I begin by saying they didn't understand me.

356
00:14:33,340 --> 00:14:34,560
But I can't say that here.

357
00:14:35,180 --> 00:14:38,380
And then he said, I can't let us explain my view better

358
00:14:38,380 --> 00:14:39,660
than I've explained it myself.

359
00:14:40,280 --> 00:14:41,820
But I love you too, Donald.

360
00:14:41,820 --> 00:14:42,120
All right.

361
00:14:42,120 --> 00:14:43,120
So take that.

362
00:14:43,120 --> 00:14:47,660
Anyone who ever claimed I didn't understand them,

363
00:14:47,660 --> 00:14:49,200
I understand people very well.

364
00:14:49,200 --> 00:14:51,540
Like a listener.

365
00:14:51,540 --> 00:14:53,500
So, any case.

366
00:14:53,500 --> 00:14:57,820
So Whitman says I can explain his view fairly

367
00:14:57,820 --> 00:15:00,060
and too better to say so than him.

368
00:15:01,500 --> 00:15:01,740
All right.

369
00:15:01,940 --> 00:15:02,940
So first thing.

370
00:15:02,940 --> 00:15:05,280
He says a lot of public choice arguments assume extreme sort

371
00:15:05,280 --> 00:15:05,940
of stupidity.

372
00:15:05,940 --> 00:15:08,440
Normally we don't say stupidity.

373
00:15:08,440 --> 00:15:09,520
We say ignorance.

374
00:15:10,220 --> 00:15:11,500
Right? Or lack of information.

375
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:14,340
But Whitman says it's really about stupidity,

376
00:15:14,340 --> 00:15:16,540
a lack of common sense, or really just lack of the,

377
00:15:16,540 --> 00:15:18,040
a violation of rational expectations.

378
00:15:19,160 --> 00:15:22,920
So why? Well, Whitman says, look, here's the real,

379
00:15:22,920 --> 00:15:24,920
the principle, the law of the country principle,

380
00:15:24,920 --> 00:15:26,520
voter ignorance is not a serious problem.

381
00:15:27,080 --> 00:15:28,560
Notice now he's saying ignorance.

382
00:15:28,920 --> 00:15:30,940
Because he's saying, look, if the problem were only ignorance,

383
00:15:30,940 --> 00:15:32,320
here's why you wouldn't have to worry about it.

384
00:15:32,880 --> 00:15:33,580
So first reason.

385
00:15:33,580 --> 00:15:37,780
Now, by the way, Whitman argues he's very lawyerly.

386
00:15:38,320 --> 00:15:40,600
So if you are defending your client who is accused

387
00:15:40,600 --> 00:15:42,400
of breaking a vase, the first thing

388
00:15:42,400 --> 00:15:44,280
to say is there never was a vase.

389
00:15:45,400 --> 00:15:47,840
Second of all, if there was a vase, my client was never

390
00:15:47,840 --> 00:15:48,860
within 100 miles of it.

391
00:15:49,360 --> 00:15:52,600
Third of all, if he was within 100 miles of it,

392
00:15:52,600 --> 00:15:54,560
it was broken before he arrived.

393
00:15:54,860 --> 00:15:56,900
Fourth of all, it wasn't broken before he arrived.

394
00:15:57,340 --> 00:15:58,520
Somebody else broke it.

395
00:15:58,880 --> 00:16:01,600
Fifth, if my client broke it, he was asked to.

396
00:16:01,600 --> 00:16:05,380
And then sixth, if he wasn't asked to, it was self-defense,

397
00:16:05,660 --> 00:16:07,760
and you just go through every one of these arguments.

398
00:16:07,760 --> 00:16:08,940
And of course, to win your case,

399
00:16:08,940 --> 00:16:10,160
you've got to disprove all of them.

400
00:16:10,920 --> 00:16:13,300
So you have to get over every one of these arguments

401
00:16:13,300 --> 00:16:14,660
in order to actually convict.

402
00:16:15,240 --> 00:16:16,640
And that's how Whitman works.

403
00:16:17,120 --> 00:16:19,360
So even if some of these arguments don't seem

404
00:16:19,360 --> 00:16:20,780
that don't seem as good as others,

405
00:16:21,580 --> 00:16:24,400
for Whitman it's really just a matter of thinning

406
00:16:24,400 --> 00:16:27,060
down the assault until finally there's nobody left at the end

407
00:16:27,060 --> 00:16:28,500
who can actually get over the walls.

408
00:16:29,720 --> 00:16:29,940
All right.

409
00:16:29,940 --> 00:16:32,360
So first principle, he says, amount of information held

410
00:16:32,360 --> 00:16:33,760
by voters has been underestimated.

411
00:16:34,260 --> 00:16:35,240
So why would he say this?

412
00:16:35,700 --> 00:16:36,920
Well, he says, look, yeah, you can go

413
00:16:36,920 --> 00:16:38,400
and give people a bunch of tasks and show

414
00:16:38,400 --> 00:16:39,360
that they don't know very much.

415
00:16:39,360 --> 00:16:41,840
But, he says, look, party labels are brand names

416
00:16:41,840 --> 00:16:43,740
that drastically economize information costs.

417
00:16:44,060 --> 00:16:45,300
You don't need to know all the details

418
00:16:45,300 --> 00:16:47,280
about what Hillary voted on or anything.

419
00:16:47,280 --> 00:16:48,900
Look, you know Hillary's a liberal.

420
00:16:48,900 --> 00:16:49,940
You know Trump's not.

421
00:16:50,560 --> 00:16:52,760
You know which one you'd rather have.

422
00:16:52,820 --> 00:16:54,680
So it's actually pretty easy.

423
00:16:55,580 --> 00:16:56,500
Right? And then furthermore,

424
00:16:56,500 --> 00:16:58,500
the cost of information is not a big deal either

425
00:16:58,500 --> 00:17:01,140
because politicians are very happy to pay

426
00:17:01,140 --> 00:17:02,880
for the information and just give it to you.

427
00:17:03,440 --> 00:17:05,040
So the fact that it takes time, you don't have

428
00:17:05,040 --> 00:17:05,820
to actually put the time in.

429
00:17:05,820 --> 00:17:06,620
It's just handed to you.

430
00:17:06,620 --> 00:17:08,940
In fact, if you don't want information during election

431
00:17:08,940 --> 00:17:11,520
season, it's very hard not to receive this information.

432
00:17:12,100 --> 00:17:12,840
You have to really hide.

433
00:17:13,980 --> 00:17:14,200
All right.

434
00:17:14,200 --> 00:17:17,020
So second, his informed judgments can be made

435
00:17:17,020 --> 00:17:18,060
with very little information.

436
00:17:18,720 --> 00:17:21,400
So he says, voters have a lot of cognitive shortcuts,

437
00:17:21,720 --> 00:17:24,660
things like just doing whatever the interest group

438
00:17:24,660 --> 00:17:25,340
that you trust says.

439
00:17:25,340 --> 00:17:26,480
So you don't like gun control,

440
00:17:26,480 --> 00:17:29,000
you just do whatever the NRA says and that solves your problem.

441
00:17:30,480 --> 00:17:30,660
All right.

442
00:17:30,720 --> 00:17:32,780
Third, he says the bad effects

443
00:17:32,780 --> 00:17:34,140
of biased information are overstated.

444
00:17:34,820 --> 00:17:35,840
So one of them is just

445
00:17:35,840 --> 00:17:37,680
because you're ignorant doesn't mean we have systematic bias.

446
00:17:37,680 --> 00:17:40,420
And he says as long as you vote randomly,

447
00:17:40,640 --> 00:17:42,940
then this miracle aggregation saves us.

448
00:17:43,720 --> 00:17:45,780
So as long as ignorance is random voting.

449
00:17:46,620 --> 00:17:47,800
And that is of course the way

450
00:17:47,800 --> 00:17:50,040
that economists almost always model ignorance.

451
00:17:50,500 --> 00:17:51,860
So Whitman here is plugging

452
00:17:51,860 --> 00:17:55,340
into a very well-established approach to ignorance.

453
00:17:55,340 --> 00:17:57,160
Just saying if people don't know what they're doing,

454
00:17:57,300 --> 00:17:57,860
they flip a coin.

455
00:17:59,060 --> 00:17:59,280
All right.

456
00:17:59,360 --> 00:18:00,380
And here is the line.

457
00:18:00,380 --> 00:18:02,480
So to be uninformed about a policy does not imply

458
00:18:02,480 --> 00:18:04,200
that voters have biased estimates of effects.

459
00:18:04,760 --> 00:18:06,300
For example, to be uninformed about the nature

460
00:18:06,300 --> 00:18:09,080
of pork barrel projects in other congressional districts does not

461
00:18:09,080 --> 00:18:10,720
mean that voters tend to underestimate the effects

462
00:18:10,720 --> 00:18:11,480
of pork barrel.

463
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:14,440
It is quite possible that the uninformed exaggerate both the

464
00:18:14,440 --> 00:18:15,620
extent and the negative consequences

465
00:18:15,620 --> 00:18:16,600
of pork barrel politics.

466
00:18:16,960 --> 00:18:20,120
As long as the people who are overly pessimistic

467
00:18:20,120 --> 00:18:22,020
and the people who are overly optimistic cancel each other

468
00:18:22,020 --> 00:18:24,840
out, then in fact the electoral outcome is the same

469
00:18:24,840 --> 00:18:26,720
as it would be if everybody knew everything.

470
00:18:27,820 --> 00:18:30,380
Furthermore, if you just say, yeah, but there's media bias.

471
00:18:30,960 --> 00:18:32,520
Well, Whitman's answer here is, well,

472
00:18:32,520 --> 00:18:33,880
do people know there's media bias?

473
00:18:34,520 --> 00:18:35,500
Do they know the direction?

474
00:18:36,220 --> 00:18:38,420
Well, in that case, there's a very simple thing to do.

475
00:18:38,760 --> 00:18:39,380
Two simple things.

476
00:18:39,760 --> 00:18:41,320
One is stop watching the biased news.

477
00:18:42,440 --> 00:18:43,640
So you don't trust liberal media?

478
00:18:43,840 --> 00:18:44,900
Stop watching liberal media.

479
00:18:45,800 --> 00:18:48,040
Right? Or you could just watch it

480
00:18:48,040 --> 00:18:49,060
for entertainment value only.

481
00:18:49,680 --> 00:18:51,920
So you could just go and say, can you believe these people?

482
00:18:52,340 --> 00:18:54,000
Fox News. You could hate watch it.

483
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:55,240
It's like, oh, come on.

484
00:18:55,560 --> 00:18:56,660
Every, every, everything you watch.

485
00:18:56,660 --> 00:18:57,280
Oh, my God.

486
00:18:57,280 --> 00:18:58,460
Another ridiculous lie.

487
00:18:58,460 --> 00:18:59,180
Another lie.

488
00:18:59,180 --> 00:19:00,240
Lies, lies, lies.

489
00:19:00,860 --> 00:19:02,640
Right? And then say, well, but since I know it,

490
00:19:02,640 --> 00:19:04,460
I know it's all lies, it doesn't actually affect me.

491
00:19:05,360 --> 00:19:07,680
Which again is the common sense thing that if you are,

492
00:19:07,680 --> 00:19:09,400
if you are listening to someone that you believe

493
00:19:09,400 --> 00:19:11,940
to be a horrible liar, they're not going to trick you

494
00:19:11,940 --> 00:19:14,820
because you ignore, either you don't listen to them

495
00:19:15,160 --> 00:19:17,200
or you just sit there saying, yes, yes, of course.

496
00:19:17,580 --> 00:19:18,400
You cured cancer.

497
00:19:18,820 --> 00:19:20,000
Yes, that's all very interesting.

498
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:20,880
You're an Olympic athlete.

499
00:19:20,880 --> 00:19:21,600
You cured cancer.

500
00:19:22,120 --> 00:19:23,720
Yes, once we did have a graduate student

501
00:19:23,720 --> 00:19:25,920
who appeared to just be a complete pathological liar.

502
00:19:26,460 --> 00:19:29,500
And finally people caught on to it.

503
00:19:29,900 --> 00:19:34,000
So not mentioning any names, but like any news story,

504
00:19:34,220 --> 00:19:35,960
this student had personal friends involved

505
00:19:35,960 --> 00:19:37,700
in the news story no matter what it was.

506
00:19:38,240 --> 00:19:41,380
Oh, yeah, my brother-in-law was actually very closely involved

507
00:19:41,380 --> 00:19:41,740
in that.

508
00:19:41,740 --> 00:19:42,620
I'm like, whatever you say.

509
00:19:42,620 --> 00:19:43,000
Oh, yeah.

510
00:19:43,160 --> 00:19:45,100
It's like, eventually.

511
00:19:45,700 --> 00:19:47,560
Right. It's just a little bit hard to believe.

512
00:19:48,980 --> 00:19:49,160
All right.

513
00:19:50,160 --> 00:19:52,420
Okay. So anyway, if the media is a liberal bias,

514
00:19:52,420 --> 00:19:53,720
then voters can easily adjust.

515
00:19:53,720 --> 00:19:55,720
So that's the answer.

